422: ## 9. The actual Cartier overlap of the two source flop chambers
423: 
424: The chamber overlap in the preceding paragraph can be made literal.
425: Write \(p'=p-G\), and let \(F_2=1-Bz^3-(1-2v)z^2\) be the actual
426: Cartier equation from Section 2. The addition formula gives
427: \[
428:  X'=\frac{F_2}{z^2},\qquad Y'=v-\frac{X'}z.
429: \]
430: Set \(A'=Y'-v,\ B'=Y'+v,\ C'=X'\), and
431: \(D'=T+X'^2+(1-2v)X'\). Direct substitution gives
432: \[
433:                    A'=-C'/z,\qquad D'=-B'/z.                \tag{SZ.14}
434: \]
435: These are exactly the equations of the other small resolution of
436: \(A'B'=C'D'\). In particular the map contracts the proper component
437: \(C_0\), where \(F_2=0\), and the ratio of the other ruling is
438: \(-1/z\). The marked target small resolution uses the first ruling.
439: This identifies the graph of translation by \(-G\) with the actual
440: Atiyah flop in these coordinates.
441: 
442: Use its common Cox coordinates
443: \[
444:  A'=s_0t_0,\quad B'=s_1t_1,\quad C'=s_0t_1,\quad D'=s_1t_0,
445: \]
446: with auxiliary weights \(+1\) on the \(s_i\) and \(-1\) on the
447: \(t_i\). The target ruling has coordinate \(s_0/s_1\), and its marked
448: section \(G\) has \(s_0=0\). The domain ruling has coordinate
449: \(t_0/t_1=-1/z\), and its zero section \(O\) has \(t_1=0\).
450: Let \(V\) have auxiliary weight \(+1\). On the target chart the
451: canonical meromorphic coefficient of the line \(O(G-O)\) is
452: \[
453:                              w_{\rm target}=V/s_0;
454: \]
455: on the domain chart it is
456: \[
457:                              w_{\rm source}=Vt_1.
458: \]
459: For clarity, on \(s_1=1\) the regular line frame is the divisor
460: frame \(1/(s_0/s_1)\), whereas on \(t_0=1\) it is the divisor
461: frame \(t_1/t_0\). These two displayed coefficients are exactly
462: their expressions in the canonical meromorphic frame. Hence
463: \[
464:                  \frac{w_{\rm target}}{w_{\rm source}}
465:                        =\frac1{s_0t_1}=\frac1{X'},           \tag{SZ.15}
466: \]
467: which is the actual multiplier in (SZ.7). Thus the source line map
468: through the flop is the identity in the common Cox coordinate \(V\),
469: not merely an abstract equality of line degrees. Fixing the nonzero
470: \(V\) gives the two moment chambers of the same \(C^4\) model.
471: For the \(a\)-th tensor power it gives the same two chambers of
472: \(C^4/\mu_a\), retaining the physical delta action already recorded.
473: 
474: The actual degree-two line has a compatible explicit Cartier map
475: to the square of the degree-one line. Namely
476: \[
477:  r(p)=X(p-G)=\frac{2vF_{21}(p)}{X(p)}
478:               =\frac{F_2}{z^2}.                            \tag{SZ.16}
479: \]
480: Its divisor is the difference between the actual \(2G-O\) divisor
481: and twice \(G-O\), on this collision neighbourhood. Multiplication
482: by \(r\) is consequently the line isomorphism
483: \(O(2G-O)\longrightarrow O(G-O)^{\otimes2}\). The expression
484: \(F_2/z^2\) retains the central component and has no extra factor of
485: \(v\). At a target point \(p'=p-G\),
486: \[
487:  F_{21}(p')=\frac{r(p')r(p)}{2v}.
488: \]
489: Transporting the actual suspension through (SZ.16) therefore gives
490: \[
491:  \frac{r(p')}{r(p)}\frac{\lambda q}{vF_{21}(p')}
492:                          =\frac{2\lambda q}{X(p')^2}.       \tag{SZ.17}
493: \]
494: 
495: Let \(U_a\) denote the tensor-power version of (SZ.7), with target
496: multiplier \(X(p-G)^{-a}\). Both allowed source/line sectors now have
497: the exact common form
498: \[
499:            B_{a,b}=2(-1)^{b+1}\lambda q\,U_a^b,
500:                        \qquad ab=2.                        \tag{SZ.18}
501: \]
502: The source Cox chamber overlap for every factor \(U_a\) is (SZ.15).
503: The only remaining comparison is consequently the periodic quotient
504: of these chambers with the exact scalar and selected \(q\)-factor
505: in (SZ.18), including its Mumford boundary and finite-root caps.
506: Its boundary must be matched with the original completed family
507: before the local link numbers of Section 8 can be summed.
508: 
509: In these same target Cox coordinates the two base functions are
510: explicit:
511: \[
512:  v=\frac{s_1t_1-s_0t_0}{2},\qquad
513:  q=(s_1-s_0)(t_0+t_1)-(s_0t_1-v)^2.                         \tag{SZ.19}
514: \]
515: Both are invariant under the physical delta action. In particular
516: the \(q\)-factor in (SZ.18) is the actual invariant polynomial
517: (SZ.19); a product construction replacing it by an independent
518: disc coordinate would omit its singular central divisor.
519: The certificate checks this identity as well as the two chamber
520: line frames. This retained equation is the local input for the
521: periodic Mumford-boundary comparison.
522: 
523: ## 10. Exact two-node base coordinates
524: 
525: There is a direct analytic coordinate map to the two-node conifold,
526: without dropping any parameter unit. Put \(A_0=1-2v\) and
527: \[
528:  \xi(T,v)=\frac{-A_0+\sqrt{A_0^2-3T}}3 ,
529: \]
530: where the square root is the unit branch equal to one at the origin.
531: It satisfies \(3\xi^2+2A_0\xi+T=0\). For \(x=X-\xi\), define
532: \[
533:  R=x\sqrt{A_0+3\xi+x},\qquad
534:  h(T,v)=v^2-A_0\xi^2-2\xi^3 .
535: \]
536: Both square roots are actual holomorphic units on a sufficiently
537: small polydisc. The source equation becomes exactly
538: \[
539:                          (Y-R)(Y+R)=h(T,v).                \tag{SZ.20}
540: \]
541: The two local critical-value curves are
542: \[
543:  T_0=v^2-2v,\qquad
544:  T_1=\frac{1+4v-(1-8v)^{3/2}}8 .
545: \]
546: The latter is the small root of the remaining discriminant factor.
547: Their tangent slopes are \(-2\) and \(+2\); hence
548: \[
549:                  q_1=T-T_0(v),\qquad q_2=T-T_1(v)
550: \]
551: are actual analytic coordinates on the base near the origin.
552: The function \(h\) vanishes simply along both curves away from their
553: intersection. The explicit formula shows its quadratic term is
554: \(-\tfrac14(T^2-4v^2)\). Analytic division therefore gives
555: \[
556:                        h=\upsilon(q_1,q_2)q_1q_2,
557:                          \qquad\upsilon(0,0)=-1/4,          \tag{SZ.21}
558: \]
559: with \(\upsilon\) holomorphic and nowhere zero after shrinking.
560: Replacing \(Y-R\) by \((Y-R)/\upsilon\) in (SZ.20) gives the literal
561: equation \(AB=q_1q_2\). This is an explicit local isomorphism, including
562: the selected \(q_1\) direction, the other \(q_2\) direction and the
563: unit \(\upsilon\). The unit has a holomorphic logarithm on the
564: polydisc, so its restriction to a source-parameter loop has zero
565: winding.
566: 
567: This supplies the two-node base chart for the chamber quotient in
568: Section 9. The global periodic quotient is still not replaced by the
569: affine conifold: its actual \(q_1\) period and the physical delta
570: character remain the data in (SZ.18).
571: 
